{
  "_readme": {
    "what": "Indian proper nouns and loanwords -> IPA, injected as <phoneme alphabet=\"ipa\"> into the en-US story narration by tools/tts.py. Hindi/Punjabi clips do NOT use this file: those voices are already native.",
    "goal": "The narrator keeps her English (diaspora) accent; the Indian names are said the way an Indian family says them. Not Sanskrit-pedantic, not anglicised.",
    "engine_limits": [
      "Google's en-US phoneme set is the American English one. Symbols outside it are not an error - the API silently drops the <phoneme> tag and reads the spelling instead. Every entry here was verified against the live API to be accepted (see the checker in the report).",
      "ASPIRATION CANNOT BE RENDERED. bh dh gh kh ph th chh jh and breathy voice (bʱ dʱ tʰ kʰ ...) are all rejected. They are written unaspirated, so Bhasuraka starts as a plain b and dharma as a plain d. This is the one class of sound the phoneme approach cannot fix.",
      "PRIMARY STRESS MAY NOT FALL ON ə. English defines schwa as unstressed, so ˈə is rejected. A stressed short Indian 'a' is therefore written ʌ (the 'cup' vowel), which is the closest American English has.",
      "Retroflexes ʈ ɖ ɳ ɽ ʂ are accepted and kept where they change the word; the American voice renders them as its nearest alveolar/post-alveolar, which still beats the dental default.",
      "Long vowels are written with ː. Indian monophthongs are written eː / oː rather than the American diphthongs eɪ / oʊ where the difference is audible."
    ],
    "format": "\"Term\": { \"ipa\": \"...\", \"note\": \"plain-English respelling, CAPS = stress\", \"unsure\": true (only where the reading is genuinely in doubt) }",
    "unpinned": "\"ipa\": null with \"needs_native_speaker\": true means nobody here could transcribe it honestly. tools/tts.py drops those terms from the matcher entirely, so the voice reads the spelling as it would anyway. That is deliberate: a confidently wrong <phoneme> sounds authoritative and nobody ever re-checks it, whereas an obviously anglicised reading is something a parent will report. Do not fill these in by guessing - get a speaker.",
    "matching": "tools/tts.py matches case-insensitively, longest term first, on word boundaries, and handles a following possessive 's itself - do not add possessive forms here. Plural forms that actually occur in the stories are listed separately."
  },

